  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Clifford Mooers was an American oilman who founded Shasta Oil Company and built a fortune through independent petroleum exploration. Before entering the Texas oil business, he prospected for gold in the Yukon, ran an Alaskan trading post and flew during the First World War. He eventually sold Shasta to Stanolind.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 92,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Contemporary reporting directly supports the founder career, early work and disposal; independent judicial records corroborate owned petroleum interests."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Clifford Mooers was an American oilman who founded Shasta Oil Company and built a fortune through independent petroleum exploration. Before entering the Texas oil business, he prospected for gold in the Yukon, ran an Alaskan trading post and flew during the First World War. He eventually sold Shasta to Stanolind.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Texas oilman Clifford Mooers rode in the horse van for part of the journey that brought King's Genius to his Pine Lake Farm near Houston. He had bought the celebrated Saddlebred stallion alongside a group of mares, adding to a serious breeding operation. A veterinarian examined the ageing horse before the purchase was completed.\n\nMooers also took an interest in preserving the breed's history. Under the American Saddlebred Horse Association, he began a project to locate and mark the graves of important horses. Assisted by trainer R. P. Glenn, he had King's Genius moved from Texas for reburial on his farm near Lexington, with a properly marked grave.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 89,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "The breed association published the detailed historical account, read in coherent indexed page32–33 passages. Direct PDF retrieval and screenshot failed, so access limits are explicit. The two paragraphs recount specific actions without attributing undocumented motives."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 108,
    "html": "<p>Texas oilman Clifford Mooers rode in the horse van for part of the journey that brought King's Genius to his Pine Lake Farm near Houston. He had bought the celebrated Saddlebred stallion alongside a group of mares, adding to a serious breeding operation. A veterinarian examined the ageing horse before the purchase was completed.</p>\r\n<p>Mooers also took an interest in preserving the breed's history. Under the American Saddlebred Horse Association, he began a project to locate and mark the graves of important horses. Assisted by trainer R. P. Glenn, he had King's Genius moved from Texas for reburial on his farm near Lexington, with a properly marked grave.</p>\r\n"
  },
